\details{
The conditional mean is \code{mu = exp(eta)} and variance is \code{theta * mu^2}.
The gamma shape is \code{1 / theta} and scale is \code{mu * theta}. Dispersion is
estimated as a family parameter on the log scale. The additional mgcv scale
parameter is fixed at one; \code{family$getTheta(TRUE)} returns dispersion.

An ordinary vector response specifies exact observations. For censoring use
a two-column matrix, following \link[mgcv:cnorm]{mgcv::cnorm}: equal entries denote an exact
observation; a second entry of \code{-Inf} or \code{Inf} denotes left or right censoring
at the first entry; otherwise the entries are ordered lower and upper bounds.
Exact observations and left thresholds must be positive. Lower bounds may
be zero. Censoring is assumed non-informative conditional on the covariates.

Prior weights multiply log-likelihood contributions (replication weights);
they do not change individual gamma dispersions. Zero weights are allowed.

Response predictions are ordinary plug-in estimates of the conditional
arithmetic mean, \code{exp(eta)}, with delta-method standard errors. They do not
include an epsilon correction for nonlinear functions of uncertain model
coefficients or integrate over new random effects. Unlike a lognormal model,
no residual-variance adjustment is needed to obtain the conditional mean.

The family includes \code{rd(mu, wt = 1, scale = 1)},
\code{qf(p, mu, wt = 1, scale = 1, lower.tail = TRUE, log.p = FALSE)}, and
\code{cdf(q, mu, wt = 1, scale = 1, logp = FALSE, lower.tail = TRUE)}.
These evaluate the latent, uncensored gamma distribution using the supplied
response-scale mean and the family's current dispersion. \code{rd} draws one
response per element of \code{mu}. \code{wt} and \code{scale} are accepted for compatibility
with mgcv: replication weights do not alter the response distribution, and
dispersion comes from \code{getTheta(TRUE)}, not the external scale argument.
\code{qf} and \code{cdf} support upper tails and log probabilities; the CDF's \code{logp}
spelling follows the mgcv CDF convention.

These functions do not apply censoring, draw model parameters, or condition
on an observation's censoring event. For a randomized quantile residual of
an observation censored to \verb{[l, u]}, draw uniformly between \code{cdf(l, mu)} and
\code{cdf(u, mu)}, then apply \code{\link[stats:qnorm]{stats::qnorm()}}. Use bounds zero or infinity for
one-sided censoring. For exact observations use \code{qnorm(cdf(y, mu))}.
}
\section{Fitting routes and NCV limitations}{

REML and ML are supported in \code{gam()}; REML, ML and fREML in ordinary
\code{bam()}; and fREML and NCV in discrete \code{bam()}. Discrete NCV requires
mgcv >= 1.9-4 and optimizes a working-model criterion. Use \code{scale = 1}
in \code{bam()} for absolute log-likelihood comparisons: mgcv 1.9-4 can
otherwise mislabel the fixed external likelihood scale.

With mgcv 1.9-4, \code{gam()} NCV and QNCV requests (also via \code{nei}
during REML) are rejected for separate upstream numerical NCV issues.
Censored responses also lose censoring information in held-out likelihoods.
These restrictions remain after complete-case filtering. Support must be
reassessed on later mgcv versions. Discrete \code{bam()} NCV uses
a separate fitting route and remains available.
}

\section{NCV neighbourhood preprocessing}{

In mgcv 1.9-4, both \code{gam()} and \code{bam()} can corrupt explicit \code{nei}
neighbourhoods when the model frame omits incomplete observations. Partial
group removal can leave stale cumulative boundaries; a set containing no
omitted index can be emptied accidentally. This shared preprocessing issue
is separate from family-specific NCV restrictions. In \code{gam()}, extended
families have no \code{presetup} hook; \code{preinitialize} and \code{initialize} run after upstream
neighbourhood validation. In \code{bam()}, early link fixing runs before the
model frame exists, while hooks receiving the processed data run after
neighbourhood validation. Neither route offers a supported family hook
for this diagnostic. An upstream validation error is not inlaws guard
coverage.

For otherwise supported NCV routes, remove incomplete observations using
all relevant response, predictor, offset and weight variables, apply subset
selection, then construct \code{nei} using the resulting data's row indices.
Complete-data neighbourhoods, correctly adjusted whole-group omissions,
default leave-one-out (\code{nei = NULL}) constructed after omission, and ordinary
REML without \code{nei} do not encounter this defect. Other documented method
restrictions still apply. In particular, complete-case filtering does not
lift an independent GAM NCV restriction. No fix in a later mgcv version is
presumed; see the family compatibility vignette for the tested versions.
}

\examples{
set.seed(12)
dat <- data.frame(x = runif(200))
mu <- exp(0.5 + sin(2 * pi * dat$x))
y <- rgamma(200, shape = 3, scale = mu / 3)
dat$yc <- cbind(y, y)
censored <- y < 0.4
dat$yc[censored, 1] <- 0.4
dat$yc[censored, 2] <- -Inf
fit <- mgcv::gam(yc ~ s(x, k = 8), data = dat,
                 family = cgamma(), method = "REML")
fit$family$getTheta(TRUE) # estimated dispersion; shape is its reciprocal
predict(fit, type = "response")
}
